WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:05.690
Just because Peter Parker's Aunt May is getting
on a bit doesn't mean she's boring in fact,

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.560
there's much more to May than just a mean
batch of wheatcakes.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.040
From her humble beginnings to her big screen
debut and everything in between, here's the

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.160
untold story of Marvel's most iconic golden
oldie.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.720
Officially, May and Ben Parker made their
canonical debut alongside Spider-Man in Amazing

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:26.340
Fantasy #15, but Spider-Man's origin story
wasn't the first time that the characters

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.390
had graced the pages of a Marvel comic.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.160
A couple of months before Spider-Man's big
entrance, Stan Lee and Steve Ditko published

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:38.649
a short story called "Goodbye to Linda Brown"
in Strange Tales #97, which featured early

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:40.680
versions of both of Peter's relatives.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.440
"Goodbye to Linda Brown" is only a few pages
long and doesn't leave much room for character

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.960
development, but both Ben and May show glimmers
of their future selves.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.620
For one, they look very similar to their Amazing
Fantasy counterparts - especially May.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.199
For some reason, the colorist decided to give
Ben a grey mustache, although Ditko clearly

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.420
didn't pencil one in.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.140
In addition, both characters spend most of
their time fretting over a teenager with secret

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.590
superpowers, which is pretty much all Aunt
May did for the first 50 issues or so of Amazing

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:10.080
Spider-Man.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.180
Luckily, things did get a little more interesting
after that.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.240
Most probably saw Aunt May in the flesh for
the first time in Sam Raimi's 2002 Spider-Man

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:23.640
movie, in which Peter Parker's doddering aunt
was played by Emmy-winning actress Rosemary

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.320
Harris.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.180
But Harris wasn't actually the first person
to bring May to life in a live-action project.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.680
That honor goes to Jeff Donnell, - and so
we’re clear Jean Marie Donnell went by her

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.440
nickname Jeff, so that explains that...

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:40.759
Anyway, she portrayed Aunt May in a 90-minute
TV movie that served as the backdoor pilot

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:43.840
for the 1977 Spider-Man television series.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.170
Donnell only played Aunt May in the Spider-Man
pilot for May's later appearances, the role

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.869
was assumed by a number of different actresses,
including recurring Dennis the Menace star

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:53.860
Irene Tedrow.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.619
And, of course, many actresses tackled the
role of Aunt May in the world of animation

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.100
before Harris immortalized her on the big
screen.

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:04.840
June Foray voiced May in Spider-Man and His
Amazing Friends, in which Spidey and company

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.380
operated out of May's Queens-based abode…
but you probably know Foray better as the

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.750
original voices of both Rocky the Flying Squirrel
and his arch-nemesis, Natasha Fatale.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:14.240
Her finest hour

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:19.480
In terms of early Spider-Man stories, you
won't find much that's more iconic than The

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.460
Amazing Spider-Man #33.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.380
This comic, titled "The Final Chapter," isn't
actually co-creator Steve Ditko's final pass

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:30.980
at Spider-Man, but it does feel like the end
of an era and puts Aunt May front and center

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:31.680
of the story.

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:37.040
As the issue opens, Aunt May lies in a hospital
bed, dying from a transfusion of Peter Parker's

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.319
radioactive blood.

00:02:38.319 --> 00:02:44.120
The only cure is an isotope known as ISO-36,
which is contained in an underwater lab controlled

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.960
by Spider-Man's arch-nemesis, Doctor Octopus.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.310
The two fight and destroy the lab, and as
it fills with water, Spider-Man is pinned

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:55.260
underneath multiple tons of fallen steel,
with no hope of escape.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.710
The following four pages are arguably Ditko's
finest work in any Spider-Man comic at least,

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:01.880
that's what Stan Lee thinks.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:02.840
Lee later said:

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.220
"[It] was one of my all time favorite stories.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.220
[...] It was such a thrill, even to me and
I was the writer of the story.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.660
When I saw that I almost shouted in triumph.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.200
Steve did a wonderful job."

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.579
And who does Peter have to thank for his big
moment?

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.240
Well, Ditko, of course, but also Aunt May.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.370
As the water rises, Spider-Man thinks about
his beloved aunt, and how he's failed her

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:26.130
in the past namely by letting an anonymous
robber get away, leaving him free to kill

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.180
her husband Uncle Ben.

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:32.380
Using May's strength and resilience as motivation,
Peter lifts the steel beams above his head

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.220
and escapes.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:37.880
He delivers the isotope to the hospital in
the nick of time and, with May cured, puts

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:41.000
the specter of Uncle Ben's death behind him
once and for all.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.780
The scene is so iconic that it plays out on
the big screen too, in Spider-Man : Homecoming.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.800
The Amazing Spider-Man #121, in which the
Green Goblin kills Peter Parker's girlfriend

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:57.670
Gwen Stacy, is arguably one of the most important
and notorious superhero comics ever published,

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.000
but it almost played out very, very differently.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.700
Originally, Gwen Stacy was going to make it
to the end of the issue alive and well while

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.040
Aunt May was to meet her demise at the Goblin's
hands instead.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.069
That revelation comes from none other than
long-time Spider-Man artist John Romita Senior,

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:15.600
who co-plotted the issue with writer Gerry
Conway.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:20.980
While talking to CBR, Romita confessed that
he, and not Conway or artist Gil Kane, was

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.580
Gwen Stacy's real "murderer."

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:27.360
The original plan was to sacrifice Aunt May
to the Green Goblin, but Romita pushed back

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.470
on the idea, claiming that it would actually
make The Amazing Spider-Man less dramatic.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.460
Romita explained:

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.680
"It'll lose one of Peter Parker's hang-ups.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.720
He won't have to worry about Aunt May anymore.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.560
He won't be treated like a child anymore.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.660
If we want to make any kind of stir in the
monthly line, we have to kill somebody important."

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:47.580
And so, Romita explained, the victim had to
be one of the story's leading female figures.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.350
From there, Gwen Stacy became the obvious
choice.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:55.140
At the time, Mary Jane was what Romita called
"an airheaded comedy character", but Gwen

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.940
Stacy was the love of Peter Parker's life.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:01.720
In order to wring the maximum amount of emotional
turmoil out of the story, Gwen had to go.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.760
And the rest, of course, is history.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.820
Aunt May's abilities aren't limited to simply
fretting about the well-being of superheroes,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:09.320
though.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.660
In fact, she's also a superhero herself.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:12.520
Sort of.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:18.060
In What If #23, a back-up feature called "What
if Aunt May instead of her nephew Peter had

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.340
been bitten by that radioactive spider?"

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.180
tells the story of well, it's kinda obvious,
isn't it?

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:28.210
In Amazing Fantasy #15, Peter attends a scientific
demonstration where he crosses paths with

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:29.980
the fateful radioactive spider.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.310
In What If #23, he leaves his bagged lunch
at home, and Aunt May rushes to the Science

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:36.540
Institute to deliver it.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:40.960
While she's there, the spider bites May on
the hand, and her spider-powers kick in on

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:41.680
the walk home.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:46.810
May quickly sews herself a costume and creates
a unique version of Spider-Man's web-fluid

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.560
out of some over-yeasted dough.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.760
Before long, she's taken to the streets as
Spider-Ma'am, juggling her crime-fighting

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.360
career with her familial responsibilities.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.960
And while What If stories aren't canonical,
writer Christos Gage and artist Dave Williams

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:05.050
made Spider-Ma'am a reality in Spider-Verse
Team-Up #3, part of Dan Slott and Olivier

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:08.100
Coipel's dimension-hopping epic, Spider-Verse.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.740
In the comic, a team of Spider-Men, including
the Peter Parker that we know and love, travel

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:18.560
to Earth-3123 to rescue Spider-Ma'am from
Karn, an interdimensional hunter with a mad

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.620
hatred for everything spider-related.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.180
The team is successful, but the battle leads
to a dramatic development in Spider-Ma'am's

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:29.190
story: she finally reveals her secret identity
to her husband and nephew…

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:30.820
whose lives are changed forever.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.460
The list of people who have stopped Galactus,
Marvel Comics' gargantuan devourer of worlds,

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:37.280
is pretty short.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:39.520
The Fantastic Four have done it, of course.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.500
So have Thanos, the Silver Surfer and the
Beyonder.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:43.700
And Aunt May.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:44.460
wait, what?

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:50.460
This tale took place in Marvel Team-Up #137,
which was part of Marvel's "Assistant Editor's

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:54.640
Month," an event during which some of Marvel's
top creators were given free reign to tell

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.420
the wildest and silliest stories they could
imagine.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:02.400
In the comic, Spider-Man and the Fantastic
Four head off to San Diego to thwart an unnamed

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.680
disaster, leaving Reed Richards and Sue Storm's
superpowered son Franklin in Aunt May's care.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.240
Unfortunately, just after the real heroes
leave town, Galactus arrives in search of

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.320
a new herald and he's got his sights set on
Franklin.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:20.280
One ill-aimed energy blast later, and Aunt
May is transformed into the Golden Oldie,

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.300
a glowing super-heroine infused with the power
cosmic.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.940
"I believe there's a hero in all of us.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:31.100
That keeps us honest, gives us strength."

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:36.560
Thanks to Galactus, all of Aunt May's health
problems her arthritis, her vitamin deficiencies,

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.320
her bingo-addiction are gone.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:43.240
But there's a catch: Galactus is hungry, and
it's up to his herald to keep him fed.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.360
At Franklin's prompting, May feeds Galactus
a Twinkie.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:48.120
He loves it, and demands more.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:53.300
Using her newfound powers, May scours first
Earth and then the entire galaxy for more

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:55.590
of the spongy, cream-filled snack cakes.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:08:00.190
On her travels, she meets an alien that looks
suspiciously like the Pillsbury Dough Boy

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:03.560
who just happens to make planets that resemble
Hostess products.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:08.560
At May's suggestion, Galactus takes the alien
on as his new herald and frees May from her

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:09.060
responsibilities.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.540
When she returns home, Franklin absorbs all
of May's cosmic powers, and everything goes

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:17.830
back to normal right before writer Michael
Carlin reveals that the whole adventure was

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.880
just a bad dream… or was it?

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.780
Peter Parker will always be the main man in
May Parker's life, but with such a busy day-to-day

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.960
life, May's nephew doesn't always have time
for his beleaguered aunt.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:33.780
Thankfully, despite her age, May is incredibly
popular with the fellas, and has accumulated

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:36.800
quite a collection of dudes to keep her company
while Peter's away.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:41.680
There was Ben Parker, of course, who met his
tragic demise in Amazing Fantasy #15.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.200
But before Ben and May got hitched, she almost
married a criminal named Johnny Jerome, who

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:49.800
tried to win her heart with money, and who
returned to plague her in The Spectacular

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.600
Spider-Man Annual #4.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.060
In The Amazing Spider-Man #54, Doctor Octopus
rents May's spare room, and wins her over

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:58.000
with his charms.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:04.740
And in The Amazing Spider-Man #131, he actually
tries to marry her in order to steal her inheritance,

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.160
although Hammerhead and Spider-Man interrupt
the wedding before it becomes official.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.139
"Cute place very homey.”

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:11.900
“Oh great, it's Liv."

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:17.060
Throughout the '80s, May dated Nathan Lubensky,
a reformed gambling addict who asked May to

00:09:17.069 --> 00:09:22.529
marry him before he relapsed, fell afoul of
the Vulture, and died in Amazing Spider-Man

00:09:22.529 --> 00:09:23.300
#336.

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:27.860
In the early '90s, she enjoyed a brief fling
with the Fantastic Four's long-time mailman

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:28.780
Willie Lumpkin.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.019
After Peter Parker and Aunt May moved to Stark
Tower in the buildup to Civil War, May and

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:35.980
Tony Stark's butler, Jarvis, shared a mutual
attraction.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.360
Finally, May dated and married J. Jonah Jameson's
father, John Jameson, Sr., and stayed with

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.480
him until he died of a rare hereditary disease
in The Amazing Spider-Man Volume 4 #19.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:53.540
Aunt May actress Rosemary Harris was 75 in
2002 when Sam Raimi's Spider-Man hit theaters.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:57.500
When she appeared as May in The Amazing Spider-Man,
Sally Field was 66.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:02.780
By contrast, Marisa Tomei, who plays May in
the modern MCU was only 50 when she first

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.420
starred as Peter Parker's favorite aunt.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.320
That's significantly younger than May is usually
portrayed—in Civil War, Tony Stark even

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:10.020
jokes about it.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:11.160
"Who else knows?

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:11.980
Anybody?”

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:14.120
“Nobody.”

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.360
“Not even your unusually attractive aunt."

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:18.220
But don't blame Tomei.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:22.430
While appearing on The Late Show with Stephen
Colbert, Tomei revealed that she actually

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:27.340
petitioned Marvel to make May into a senior
citizen, just like in the comics but the studio

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:27.880
declined.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:29.980
"I actually made a case to go with..

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:31.379
To age me up.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.020
But no they didn't do it."

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:38.460
Besides, as Civil War co-writer Stephen McFeely
has explained, a younger Aunt May makes perfect

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:41.900
sense, given that Tom Holland's Peter Parker
is still in high school.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:44.180
McFeely told the Los Angeles Times:

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.680
"That's partly why his aunt isn't 80 years
old.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.720
If she's the sister of his dead mother, why
does she have to be two generations ahead?"

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:55.519
In other words, the MCU's Aunt May isn't too
young at all in the comics, she's actually

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:56.580
way too old.

